<node id="Expression::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_6">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_6</data>
  <data key="d2">\text{TOW} = \text{TCD} + \text{rain period}^a</data>
  <data key="d5">\text{TOW} = \text{TCD} + \text{rain period}^a</data>
  <data key="d6">Total time of wetness (TOW) is approximated by the time of condensation (TCD) plus rain periods.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_6">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_6</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3, Eq [1]</data>
  <data key="d7">TOW = TCD + rain perioda [1]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 3, Eq [1]</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_7">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_7</data>
  <data key="d2">\text{TCD} = \text{time when TCON} &gt; 0</data>
  <data key="d5">\text{TCD} = \text{time when TCON} &gt; 0</data>
  <data key="d6">Time of condensation (TCD) is defined as the time when the tendency to condensation (TCON) is greater than zero.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_7">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_7</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3, Eq [2]</data>
  <data key="d7">TCD = time when TCON &gt; 0 [2]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 3, Eq [2]</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_8">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_8</data>
  <data key="d2">\text{TCON} = V_a - V_s</data>
  <data key="d5">\text{TCON} = V_a - V_s</data>
  <data key="d6">Tendency to condensation (TCON) is the difference between the vapor concentration of the air (Va) and the saturation vapor concentration of the surface (Vs).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_8">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_8</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3, Eq [3]</data>
  <data key="d7">TCON = Va - Vs [3]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 3, Eq [3]</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_9">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_9</data>
  <data key="d2">V_s = (RH/100) \exp(22.565 - 2377.1T_s^{-1} - 33623T_s^{-1.5})</data>
  <data key="d5">V_s = (RH/100) \exp(22.565 - 2377.1T_s^{-1} - 33623T_s^{-1.5})</data>
  <data key="d6">Saturation vapor concentration of the surface (Vs) as a function of surface temperature (Ts) and relative humidity (RH).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_9">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_9</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3, Eq [4]</data>
  <data key="d7">Vs = (RH/100)exp(22.565 – 2377.1T_s^{-1} – 33623T_s^{-1.5}) [4]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 3, Eq [4]</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_10">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_10</data>
  <data key="d2">V_a = V_{as} \text{RH}/100</data>
  <data key="d5">V_a = V_{as} \text{RH}/100</data>
  <data key="d6">Vapor pressure of the air (Va) as a function of saturation vapor pressure of the adjacent air (Vas) and relative humidity (RH).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_10">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_10</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 4, Eq [5]</data>
  <data key="d7">Va = VasRH/100 [5]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 4, Eq [5]</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_11">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::expr_11</data>
  <data key="d2">\text{RH}_s = \text{DRH}</data>
  <data key="d5">\text{RH}_s = \text{DRH}</data>
  <data key="d6">A reasonable approximation is to set the relative humidity required for wetting (RHs) equal to the deliquescent relative humidity (DRH) of contaminating salts.</data>
</node>
<node id="OperatorFamily::THRESH">
  <data key="d0">OperatorFamily</data>
  <data key="d1">THRESH</data>
  <data key="d2">THRESH</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_11">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_11</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 4, Eq [7]</data>
  <data key="d7">A reasonable approximation is to set RH, equal to the DRH of contaminating salts, i.e. RHs = DRH [7]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 4, Eq [7]</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_577685482">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_577685482</data>
  <data key="d2">V_s = f(\text{RH}, T_s)</data>
  <data key="d6">Saturation vapor concentration of the surface (Vs) is a function of both relative humidity (RH) and surface temperature (Ts).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_678419224">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_678419224</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3, Eq [4]</data>
  <data key="d7">Vs = (RH/100)exp(22.565 – 2377.1T_s^{-1} – 33623T_s^{-1.5}) [4]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 3, Eq [4]</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_511060761">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_511060761</data>
  <data key="d2">V_a = f(V_{as}(T_a), \text{RH})</data>
  <data key="d6">Vapor concentration of the air (Va) is a function of saturation vapor pressure of the adjacent air (Vas), which depends on air temperature (Ta), and ambient relative humidity (RH).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_813081570">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_813081570</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 4, Eq [5], [6]</data>
  <data key="d7">Va = VasRH/100 [5] where Vas is the saturation vapor pressure (in pascals) of the adjacent air, which is given by Vas = exp(22.565 – 2377.1T_a^{-1} – 33623T_a^{-1.5}) [6]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 4, Eq [5], [6]</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_48837075">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_48837075</data>
  <data key="d2">\text{Wetting condition} = (\text{RH} \ge \text{DRH})</data>
  <data key="d6">The condition for surface wetting is when the relative humidity (RH) equals or exceeds the deliquescent relative humidity (DRH) of contaminating salts.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_857897552">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_857897552</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 4, Eq [7]</data>
  <data key="d7">A reasonable approximation is to set RH, equal to the DRH of contaminating salts, i.e. RHs = DRH [7]</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 4, Eq [7]</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_869504556">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::cpl_869504556</data>
  <data key="d2">\text{DRH} = f(\text{salt composition})</data>
  <data key="d6">The deliquescent relative humidity (DRH) is a property of the specific salt composition on the surface, which can include chloride-containing salts.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_45779631">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Journal of The Electrochemical Society, 151 (12) B627-B635 (2004)::ev_cpl_45779631</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 2, Abstract</data>
  <data key="d7">This paper explores the simple principle that a metal surface wets when the surface relative humidity (RH) exceeds the deliquescent RH (DRH) of any salts on the surface.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 2, Abstract</data>
</node>
