<node id="Expression::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::expr_27">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::expr_27</data>
  <data key="d2">O_{2} + 2H_{2}O + 4e^{-} \rightarrow 4OH^{-}</data>
  <data key="d5">O_{2} + 2H_{2}O + 4e^{-} \rightarrow 4OH^{-}</data>
  <data key="d6">Cathodic reaction of oxygen reduction in the electrolyte layer.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_27">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_27</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 12, Section 4.1, Eq (3)</data>
  <data key="d7">dissolved oxygen in the electrolyte layer is reduced to equilibrate anodic Reaction (2), where the cathodic reaction is depicted in Reaction (3). O2 + 2H2O + 4e− → 4OH−</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 12, Section 4.1, Eq (3)</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::expr_28">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::expr_28</data>
  <data key="d2">4Fe(OH)^{+} + 4OH^{-} + O_{2} \rightarrow 4\gamma-FeOOH + 2H_{2}O</data>
  <data key="d5">4Fe(OH)^{+} + 4OH^{-} + O_{2} \rightarrow 4\gamma-FeOOH + 2H_{2}O</data>
  <data key="d6">Transformation of initial corrosion product into stable rust phases with oxygen consumption.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_28">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_28</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 13, Section 4.1, Eq (7)</data>
  <data key="d7">the initial corrosion product is transformed into relatively stable rust phases immediately according to reaction (7). 4Fe(OH)++4OH− + O2 → 4γ-FeOOH + 2H2O</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 13, Section 4.1, Eq (7)</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::expr_29">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::expr_29</data>
  <data key="d2">4\gamma-Fe OH OH + O_{2} \rightarrow 4\gamma-FeOOH + 2H_{2}O</data>
  <data key="d5">4\gamma-Fe OH OH + O_{2} \rightarrow 4\gamma-FeOOH + 2H_{2}O</data>
  <data key="d6">Reoxidation of reduced lepidocrocite to lepidocrocite under sufficient oxygen.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_29">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_29</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 13, Section 4.1, Eq (9)</data>
  <data key="d7">the “reduced lepidocrocite” can be reoxidized to lepidocrocite under conditions of sufficient oxygen... 4γ-Fe OH OH + O2 → 4γ-FeOOH + 2H2O</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 13, Section 4.1, Eq (9)</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::cpl_35219959">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::cpl_35219959</data>
  <data key="d2">Chloride ions \times periodic wet-dry cycles</data>
  <data key="d6">Corrosive media containing chloride ions and periodic wet-dry cycles synergistically trigger pitting corrosion by forming micro-batteries and concentration cells.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_cpl_825594347">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_cpl_825594347</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 13, Section 4.1</data>
  <data key="d7">under the dual action of corrosive media containing chloride ions and periodic wet-dry cycles, the formation of micro-batteries and concentration cells triggers the pitting corrosion of steel.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 13, Section 4.1</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::cpl_460308225">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::cpl_460308225</data>
  <data key="d2">Cl content \times rust layer properties</data>
  <data key="d6">Under the synergistic effect of a small concentration gradient between the electrolyte at the outside and inside of the rust layer, higher Cl content and the accumulation of Fe3+ and Fe2+ resulted in a lower pH and anodic dissolution of steel.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_cpl_857024922">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_cpl_857024922</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 14, Section 4.2</data>
  <data key="d7">Under the synergistic effect of a small concentration gradient between the electrolyte at the outside and inside of the rust layer, higher Cl content and the accumulation of Fe3+ and Fe2+ resulted in a lower pH and anodic dissolution of steel through Reactions (5)–(6) and (15)–(16).</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 14, Section 4.2</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::cpl_802904501">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::cpl_802904501</data>
  <data key="d2">wet-dry state \times RH \times temperature \times salty aerosols</data>
  <data key="d6">Changes in the wet-dry state of the surface of steel exposed to the atmospheric zone are mainly controlled by the condensation of salty aerosols affected by RH and temperature, as well as water condensation.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_cpl_84187157">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.154864::ev_cpl_84187157</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 14, Section 4.2</data>
  <data key="d7">Changes in the wet-dry state of the surface of steel exposed to the atmospheric zone are mainly controlled by the condensation of salty aerosols affected by RH and temperature, as well as water condensation.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 14, Section 4.2</data>
</node>
