<node id="Evidence::2009_Ma_Corrosion_Science.pdf::ev_42">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">2009_Ma_Corrosion_Science.pdf::ev_42</data>
  <data key="d2">Section 3.2, Equation (2)</data>
  <data key="d7">The atmospheric corrosion behavior of carbon steel specimens exposed to the industrial site is observed to follow the well-known bilogarithmic equation: C = At^B</data>
  <data key="d8">Section 3.2, Equation (2)</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::2009_Ma_Corrosion_Science.pdf::expr_43">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">2009_Ma_Corrosion_Science.pdf::expr_43</data>
  <data key="d2">C = A * t_1^(B1-B2) * t^B2</data>
  <data key="d5">C = A * t_1^(B1-B2) * t^B2</data>
  <data key="d6">Equation describing irregular corrosion behavior in the marine site, where C is thickness loss, t is time, and t1 is the turning point. Constants B1 and B2 are influenced by Cl- deposition.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::2009_Ma_Corrosion_Science.pdf::ev_43">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">2009_Ma_Corrosion_Science.pdf::ev_43</data>
  <data key="d2">Section 3.2, Equation (3)</data>
  <data key="d7">The representative equation of this irregular corrosion behavior can be described as [19]; C = At_1^(B1-B2)t^B2 (t ≥ t₁)</data>
  <data key="d8">Section 3.2, Equation (3)</data>
</node>
