<node id="Coupling::10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::cpl_111705302">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::cpl_111705302</data>
  <data key="d2">SO2 and Cl-</data>
  <data key="d6">Possible synergistic effect of SO2 and Cl- on metal corrosion.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::ev_cpl_768775852">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::ev_cpl_768775852</data>
  <data key="d2">Section 2.3, page 3</data>
  <data key="d7">considering the possible synergistic effect of SO2 and Cl- on the metals</data>
  <data key="d8">Section 2.3, page 3</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::cpl_240256671">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::cpl_240256671</data>
  <data key="d2">rcorr = f(temperature, relative humidity, SO2, Cl-)</data>
  <data key="d6">Corrosion losses over the first year (rcorr) are calculated using dose-response functions that take into account yearly average temperature, relative humidity, SO2, and Cl-.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::ev_cpl_765780720">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">10.1016/j.corsci.2016.04.002::ev_cpl_765780720</data>
  <data key="d2">Section 3.3.1, page 8</data>
  <data key="d7">rcorr means corrosion losses over the first year, the values of which for any world locations can be calculated using the developed dose-response functions [29] that take into account the yearly average temperature and relative humidity as well as the yearly average precipitation of SO2 and Cl¯.</data>
  <data key="d8">Section 3.3.1, page 8</data>
</node>
