<node id="Expression::maco.202314201::expr_126">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::expr_126</data>
  <data key="d2">c(t) = At^B</data>
  <data key="d5">c(t) = At^B</data>
  <data key="d6">Power law representing corrosion loss c(t) as a function of time t, where A and B are constants. This model is derived from diffusion mathematics under time-dependent increase in diffusion resistance.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::maco.202314201::ev_126">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::ev_126</data>
  <data key="d2">p. 2, Eq. 1</data>
  <data key="d7">that the gradual decline of the rate of corrosion of metals in the atmosphere may be represented by a power law: c(t) = AtB</data>
  <data key="d8">p. 2, Eq. 1</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::maco.202314201::expr_127">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::expr_127</data>
  <data key="d2">O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -&gt; 4OH-</data>
  <data key="d5">O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -&gt; 4OH-</data>
  <data key="d6">Cathodic oxygen reduction reaction, where its rate is limited by the availability of dissolved oxygen diffusing to the metal surface. This is the controlling cathodic reaction in Mode 1.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::maco.202314201::ev_127">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::ev_127</data>
  <data key="d2">p. 4, Eq. 3</data>
  <data key="d7">the relevant reaction is the (cathodic) oxygen reduction reaction: O2 + 2H2O + 4e¯ → 4OН¯</data>
  <data key="d8">p. 4, Eq. 3</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::maco.202314201::expr_128">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::expr_128</data>
  <data key="d2">2H2O + 2e- -&gt; 2OH- + H2</data>
  <data key="d5">2H2O + 2e- -&gt; 2OH- + H2</data>
  <data key="d6">Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), which becomes the main cathodic reaction in Mode 2, with its rate controlled by hydrogen diffusion outwards through the corrosion products.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::maco.202314201::ev_128">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::ev_128</data>
  <data key="d2">p. 5, Eq. 5</data>
  <data key="d7">the so-called hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). ... In its most basic form, it may be summarised as: 2H2O + 2e- → 2OH¯ + H₂ ↑</data>
  <data key="d8">p. 5, Eq. 5</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::maco.202314201::cpl_986103243">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::cpl_986103243</data>
  <data key="d2">Qualitative: Chlorides increase time of wetness, enabling transport-limited reactions.</data>
  <data key="d6">In atmospheric corrosion, chlorides, due to their hygroscopic nature, increase the time of wetness. This extends the period during which water is available for the cathodic reactions (both oxygen reduction and hydrogen evolution), which are fundamentally transport-controlled processes.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::maco.202314201::ev_cpl_907162734">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::ev_cpl_907162734</data>
  <data key="d2">p. 6</data>
  <data key="d7">For atmospheric corrosion, the chlorides as sodium chlorides are likely, because of their hygroscopic nature, to contribute to keeping metal surfaces and corrosion products wet for longer periods of time...</data>
  <data key="d8">p. 6</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::maco.202314201::cpl_249412403">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::cpl_249412403</data>
  <data key="d2">ro(T), ta(T), Ca(T), Cs(T), rs(T)</data>
  <data key="d6">Temperature directly influences the kinetic parameters (initial rate, transition time, corrosion loss coefficients) of the bimodal corrosion model, which describes the evolution of diffusion-controlled corrosion processes over time.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::maco.202314201::ev_cpl_559569284">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">maco.202314201::ev_cpl_559569284</data>
  <data key="d2">p. 6</data>
  <data key="d7">The way mean seawater temperature influences the parameters of the bimodal model (Figure 1) is summarised in Table 2, based on a wide collection of field data.</data>
  <data key="d8">p. 6</data>
</node>
