<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::cpl_740452864">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::cpl_740452864</data>
  <data key="d2">Temperature and humidity in an alternating state</data>
  <data key="d6">When temperature decreases and humidity increases, corrosion current sharply increases due to condensation and thin liquid film formation.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::ev_cpl_296078141">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::ev_cpl_296078141</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3, Section 3.2</data>
  <data key="d7">when the temperature and humidity are in an alternating state, especially when the temperature is decreased and the humidity is increased, the corrosion current will increase sharply.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 3, Section 3.2</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::cpl_197138197">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::cpl_197138197</data>
  <data key="d2">Cl diffusion leading to increased rust layer hygroscopicity</data>
  <data key="d6">Continuous diffusion of Cl leads to β-FeOOH formation, which increases the internal stress and space within the rust layer, thereby increasing its hygroscopicity and reducing corrosion resistance at critical humidity.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::ev_cpl_542677673">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Communications 19 (2025) 63-75::ev_cpl_542677673</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 10, Section 3.5</data>
  <data key="d7">The increased space increases the hygroscopicity of rust layer, which reduces the steel corrosion of critical humidity.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 10, Section 3.5</data>
</node>
