  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">ma18194462::cpl_421908653</data>
  <data key="d2">Qualitative: High chloride concentration and sufficient oxygen supply throughout the thin liquid film are required for akaganeite formation.</data>
  <data key="d6">This describes a qualitative interaction where both high chloride availability (environment_Cl) and efficient oxygen transport through the electrolyte (transport_H) are simultaneously necessary for the formation of akaganeite.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::ma18194462::ev_cpl_855735257">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">ma18194462::ev_cpl_855735257</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 2</data>
  <data key="d7">sufficiently high chloride concentration [15,25,26] and oxygen supply throughout the thin liquid film to oxidize green rust [13,26–28] are required for akaganeite formation.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 2</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::ma18194462::cpl_798635721">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">ma18194462::cpl_798635721</data>
  <data key="d2">Qualitative: The volume of a stable droplet depends on the amount of NaCl particles pre-deposited and the relative humidity of the environment.</data>
  <data key="d6">This indicates a qualitative coupling where the size of the electrolyte droplet (related to transport_H) is influenced by both the initial salt load (environment_Cl) and the ambient humidity (transport_H).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::ma18194462::ev_cpl_42080219">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">ma18194462::ev_cpl_42080219</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 8</data>
  <data key="d7">Consequently, the volume of a stable droplet depends on the amount of NaCl particles pre-deposited and the relative humidity of the environment.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 8</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::ma18194462::cpl_395019640">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">ma18194462::cpl_395019640</data>
  <data key="d2">Qualitative: As the electrolyte evaporates, the concentration of chloride in the liquid increases.</data>
  <data key="d6">This describes a qualitative relationship where the reduction in electrolyte volume/thickness (related to transport_H, due to evaporation) leads to an increase in chloride concentration (environment_Cl).</data>
</node>
