<node id="Expression::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::expr_176">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::expr_176</data>
  <data key="d2">ΔW = ANⁿ</data>
  <data key="d5">ΔW = ANⁿ</data>
  <data key="d6">Empirical equation for atmospheric corrosion kinetics, where ΔW is weight gain, N is CCT number, and A and n are constants.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_176">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_176</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 2, Equation 1</data>
  <data key="d7">The corrosion kinetics of steel in atmosphere has been found to follow an empirical equation, according to previous results [28–30]: ΔW = ANⁿ</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 2, Equation 1</data>
</node>
<node id="Expression::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::expr_177">
  <data key="d0">Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::expr_177</data>
  <data key="d2">log ΔW = log A + n log N</data>
  <data key="d5">log ΔW = log A + n log N</data>
  <data key="d6">Logarithmic form of the corrosion kinetics equation, known as the bilogarithmic law, used for fitting experimental data.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_177">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_177</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 2, Equation 2</data>
  <data key="d7">Eq. (1) often takes the logarithmic form, which is called the bilograrithmic law... expressed as: log ΔW = log A + n log N</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 2, Equation 2</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::cpl_172266151">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::cpl_172266151</data>
  <data key="d2">Rust layer thickness/compactness (transport_H) affects Cl⁻ concentration (environment_Cl) and phase formation</data>
  <data key="d6">As the rust layer grows thicker, it acts as a diffusion barrier, reducing the supply of fresh Cl⁻ to the inner regions. This change in local Cl⁻ concentration dictates which rust phases can form, shifting from β-FeOOH in high-Cl⁻ conditions to α-FeOOH in low-Cl⁻ conditions.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_cpl_50150711">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_cpl_50150711</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 8, Section 4.1</data>
  <data key="d7">When the thickness of the rust layer reached a critical value, the concentration of Cl⁻ in the inner rust was not enough for the formation of β-FeOOH, so only α-FeOOH was formed.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 8, Section 4.1</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::cpl_827217692">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::cpl_827217692</data>
  <data key="d2">Cl⁻ concentration (environment_Cl) influences rust layer structure (transport_H) and corrosion rate</data>
  <data key="d6">High Cl⁻ concentration at the early stage leads to the formation of β-FeOOH, which has low compactness, resulting in a porous rust layer and a high corrosion rate (n=0.92). The subsequent formation of compact α-FeOOH nanograins in the inner layer improves corrosion resistance and slows the corrosion rate (n=0.54).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_cpl_761398266">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science 177 (2020) 108997::ev_cpl_761398266</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 9, Section 4.3</data>
  <data key="d7">At the early corrosion period, the rust layer consisted only of β-FeOOH. Due to the high reduction efficiency [50] and low compactness [31] of β-FeOOH, WS presented a high corrosion rate.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 9, Section 4.3</data>
</node>
