  <data key="d1">Kinetics of atmospheric corrosion of mild steel, zinc, galvanized iron and aluminium at 10 exposure stations in India::expr_191</data>
  <data key="d2">log C = log K + n log t</data>
  <data key="d5">log C = log K + n log t</data>
  <data key="d6">Logarithmic form of the general empirical equation, used for linear regression analysis.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Kinetics of atmospheric corrosion of mild steel, zinc, galvanized iron and aluminium at 10 exposure stations in India::ev_191">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Kinetics of atmospheric corrosion of mild steel, zinc, galvanized iron and aluminium at 10 exposure stations in India::ev_191</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 6, Eq. (2)</data>
  <data key="d7">Townsend and Zoccola [42] in 1982, used linear regression analysis to fit straight line to a log-log plot of corrosion loss (C) vs time (t) in logarithmic form log C = log K + n log t</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 6, Eq. (2)</data>
</node>
