<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_428160462">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_428160462</data>
  <data key="d2">hygroscopic magnesium chloride in sea-salt or sea mist enables corrosion to take place on iron and steel at much lower relative humidity</data>
  <data key="d6">The presence of hygroscopic chloride compounds (environment_Cl) lowers the critical relative humidity (transport_H) required for corrosion.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_538943721">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_538943721</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 1</data>
  <data key="d7">Evans demonstrated that the presence of hygroscopic magnesium chloride in sea-salt or sea mist enables corrosion to take place on iron and steel at much lower relative humidity than if only sodium</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 1</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_24291808">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_24291808</data>
  <data key="d2">synergistic effect of sodium chloride and SO2 at 90% RH increased the corrosion rate</data>
  <data key="d6">The combined presence of sodium chloride (environment_Cl) and SO2 at high relative humidity (transport_H) synergistically increases the corrosion rate.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_172083170">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_172083170</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 2</data>
  <data key="d7">He reported that the synergistic effect of sodium chloride and SO2 at 90% RH increased the corrosion rate of mild steel by about 14 times that caused by sodium chloride alone.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 2</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_588715907">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_588715907</data>
  <data key="d2">As the rust layer grows in thickness, the supply of fresh chloride ions from the electrolyte at the air-rust interface to the metal-rust interface may gradually diminish.</data>
  <data key="d6">The increasing rust layer thickness (transport_H) impedes the transport and supply of fresh chloride ions (environment_Cl) to the corrosion front.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_767857474">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_767857474</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 14</data>
  <data key="d7">As the rust layer grows in thickness, the supply of fresh chloride ions from the electrolyte at the air-rust interface to the metal-rust interface may gradually diminish.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 14</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_611061747">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_611061747</data>
  <data key="d2">Continued formation of these chlorides is dependant on the presence of a continuous supply of fresh chloride ions from the electrolyte at the air-corrosion product interface, and may gradually diminish as the rust layer thickens.</data>
  <data key="d6">The ongoing formation of iron chlorides (environment_Cl) is dependent on the continuous supply of chloride ions, which is hindered by a thickening rust layer (transport_H).</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_6064993">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_6064993</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 15</data>
  <data key="d7">Continued formation of these chlorides is dependant on the presence of a continuous supply of fresh chloride ions from the electrolyte at the air-corrosion product interface, and may gradually diminish as the rust layer thickens.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 15</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_315235076">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::cpl_315235076</data>
  <data key="d2">As blisters grow to form a thick continuous layer of corrosion products, the supply of chloride ions and subsequently the formation of iron chlorides will eventually dwindle compared to that during the initial stages.</data>
  <data key="d6">The growth of blisters into a thick corrosion product layer (transport_H) reduces the supply of chloride ions (environment_Cl), thereby diminishing the formation of iron chlorides.</data>
</node>
<node id="Evidence::Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_350330002">
  <data key="d0">Evidence</data>
  <data key="d1">Corrosion Science, Vol. 32. No. 4, pp. 417-432. 1991::ev_cpl_350330002</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 16</data>
  <data key="d7">As blisters grow to form a thick continuous layer of corrosion products, the supply of chloride ions and subsequently the formation of iron chlorides will eventually dwindle compared to that during the initial stages.</data>
  <data key="d8">Page 16</data>
</node>
